// Chase: original stories written for this game. Format: docs/v3/BLUEPRINT-FORMAT.md.

== chase.f01 | feature | real-time
title: Seven Miles Of Guns
genre: chase + historical-drama
tone: Pre-dawn, breath-held and exact
era: 1862 | period
setting: A blockaded southern harbour, from the wharf to the open sea | fictional | sea
logline: An enslaved harbour pilot who knows every signal, shoal and sentry on seven miles of fortified water puts on his captain's straw hat at three in the morning, takes the army's own steamer, and has until sunrise to get sixteen people past five forts.
premise: The white officers have gone ashore for the night against regulations. The crew that remains is enslaved: {abram}, the wheelman, who does the captain's work for a sixth of a deckhand's wage; an engineer; five hands. Their families are hidden aboard another vessel upriver. Each fort must be passed at the right speed, with the right whistle, by a figure who stands at the wheelhouse window the way the captain stands. Beyond the last fort is a blockading fleet that fires on anything coming out.
synopsis: The film is the run, close to real time, and each stage is a different kind of fear. Stage one is theft without hurry: steam is raised at the usual hour, the flags go up as on any morning, and the steamer backs out past the general's headquarters fifty yards away. {abram} has told the crew that if they are stopped they will hold hands and go down with the ship; the engineer, {jolly}, has the powder ready. Stage two is the pickup: a detour upriver to a dark wharf where women and children wait, among them {abram}'s wife {hester}, who agreed to this with the words that it was a risk, but that she would go where he went. A child will not stop crying. Stage three is the forts. The film counts them. At each, {abram} gives the pass signal on the whistle and keeps to the captain's lazy speed, when everything in him wants to run. He wears the captain's hat and jacket and leans on the window sill with his arms folded as the captain does; in the half dark a sentry sees a silhouette he has seen a hundred mornings. The last and greatest fort is passed at first light, the dangerous hour, since a face can now be seen. He gives the whistle: two long, one short. A pause the length of the film's whole tension. The sentry calls for them to give the enemy hell. Then, out of range, {abram} does not turn along the coast as the ship always does; he steers straight out. The fort understands, too late for its guns. Stage four is the one nobody planned well enough: the blockading fleet sees an enemy vessel bearing down out of the mist and runs out its guns. {hester} has a bedsheet. It goes up the staff, and for a long moment in the fog nobody on the warship can see what colour it is. A gunner has the lanyard in his hand. Then the sun reaches it. {abram} takes off the hat and calls across the water that he has brought some of the old government's guns. The closing card says what he was paid for the ship, and what he did with the rest of his life, in two lines.
question: How long can you perform the man who owns you, and what is on the other side of the performance?
themes: knowledge as the only property a slave could not be denied; freedom taken by skill and nerve; a family as a crew
audience: General; adults and teenagers; anyone who likes suspense with something at stake
viewpoint: With {abram} in the wheelhouse; below decks with {hester} when the tension needs air
stakes: Sixteen lives, among them five children; death by the forts' guns, or their own powder, or a rope
conflict: A pilot's cool against five garrisons, a rising sun and, at the last, the guns of the side he is running to
ending: They pass every fort, are nearly sunk by their rescuers, and are free by sunrise, with a warship as a gift.
experience: One sustained held breath, released in a sunrise
look: Blue-black water, fort lanterns, a pale straw hat in a wheelhouse window; fog turning gold
sound: Paddle wheels at a deliberately lazy beat, a steam whistle's code, a child hushed. A bedsheet snapping in the wind.
m: escape | system | safety-vs-freedom | triumph
@ abram | Abram Sills | lead | protagonist | human | he/him | 23 | His family out, tonight | His wife and children can be sold any morning | Five forts know the ship and its captain's habits | Self-contained, exact, daring by calculation | From a man playing his master to a man under his own name | Low, even; calls depths and bearings | A captain's straw hat and jacket not his own
@ hester | Hester | co-lead | ally | human | she/her | 28 | The children quiet and alive | She said she would go where he went | She can do nothing but wait below, until the sheet | Steady, dry, braver than the men think | From passenger to the one who saves them at the end | Short, practical, a hummed hymn | Headscarf, a baby, a folded bedsheet
@ jolly | Jolly | supporting | ally | human | he/him | 35 | Steam when asked, and no heroics | He keeps the engine and the powder | He has a match in his pocket and orders to use it | Gruff, loyal, fatalistic | From doubter to laughing at the rail | Mutters to the engine | Oil-black to the elbow, a rag round his neck
@ the_sentry | The Sentry | featured | antagonist | human | he/him | 19 | His relief and his coffee | He has seen this boat every morning | It is nearly light enough to see a face | Bored, sleepy, friendly | Unchanged; he waves them through | A shout across water | Grey coat, a lantern, a yawn
~ abram - hester | husband and wife | "I will go where you go" | a bedsheet run up at the last second
~ abram - jolly | wheelman and engineer | an order about powder | the match thrown overboard
~ abram - the_sentry | impostor and watcher | a silhouette | a cheer from the wall, and a boat that does not turn
